1
00:00:11,750 --> 00:00:12,180
Autumn,

2
00:00:12,180 --> 00:00:13,140
1918

3
00:00:13,140 --> 00:00:15,340
Newspapers told of people who went to work in

4
00:00:15,340 --> 00:00:15,780
the morning,

5
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:17,403
and were gone by nightfall

6
00:00:17,553 --> 00:00:19,263
What killed them was so small that no

7
00:00:19,263 --> 00:00:21,394
microscope of the time could see it

8
00:00:21,544 --> 00:00:23,894
It infected around five hundred million people

9
00:00:23,894 --> 00:00:26,494
One in every three people on Earth fell ill

10
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:28,270
It took more lives than the whole of the

11
00:00:28,270 --> 00:00:29,150
First World War,

12
00:00:29,150 --> 00:00:30,596
in about two years

13
00:00:30,746 --> 00:00:32,416
And it did something flu rarely does:

14
00:00:32,416 --> 00:00:34,765
it hit the young and the healthy hardest

15
00:00:34,915 --> 00:00:36,225
They called it the Spanish flu

16
00:00:36,225 --> 00:00:38,825
But it almost certainly did not start in Spain

17
00:00:39,022 --> 00:00:40,252
So where did it come from?

18
00:00:40,252 --> 00:00:42,252
Why did a parade turn into a disaster?

19
00:00:42,252 --> 00:00:44,252
And why did one island lose no one at

20
00:00:44,252 --> 00:00:44,532
all,

21
00:00:44,532 --> 00:00:46,852
while the island next door lost nearly a fifth

22
00:00:46,852 --> 00:00:48,612
of its people?

23
00:00:51,383 --> 00:00:52,213
Camp Funston,

24
00:00:52,213 --> 00:00:52,813
Kansas,

25
00:00:52,813 --> 00:00:53,413
USA

26
00:00:53,413 --> 00:00:53,813
March,

27
00:00:53,813 --> 00:00:54,933
1918

28
00:00:55,772 --> 00:00:57,722
More than fifty thousand recruits are packed

29
00:00:57,722 --> 00:00:59,242
into rows of wooden barracks,

30
00:00:59,242 --> 00:01:01,842
waiting to ship out to the war in Europe

31
00:01:02,077 --> 00:01:02,547
One morning,

32
00:01:02,547 --> 00:01:04,827
a cook named Albert Gitchell reports to the

33
00:01:04,827 --> 00:01:05,467
infirmary:

34
00:01:05,467 --> 00:01:05,867
fever,

35
00:01:05,867 --> 00:01:06,667
sore throat,

36
00:01:06,667 --> 00:01:07,787
headache

37
00:01:08,014 --> 00:01:08,524
By noon,

38
00:01:08,524 --> 00:01:10,324
more than a hundred others have come in

39
00:01:10,324 --> 00:01:11,004
Within days,

40
00:01:11,004 --> 00:01:13,208
the count passes five hundred

41
00:01:13,358 --> 00:01:14,348
It strikes fast,

42
00:01:14,348 --> 00:01:16,148
a high fever for a few days,

43
00:01:16,148 --> 00:01:16,988
and most recover

44
00:01:16,988 --> 00:01:19,588
The soldiers call it the three-day fever

45
00:01:19,980 --> 00:01:21,850
Gitchell is often named as one of the first

46
00:01:21,850 --> 00:01:22,610
recorded cases

47
00:01:22,610 --> 00:01:24,330
But where the virus really began,

48
00:01:24,330 --> 00:01:25,690
no one knows for sure,

49
00:01:25,690 --> 00:01:27,130
even today

50
00:01:27,368 --> 00:01:29,118
One theory says rural Kansas

51
00:01:29,118 --> 00:01:30,278
Another says France

52
00:01:30,278 --> 00:01:32,038
Another says China

53
00:01:32,283 --> 00:01:33,993
What we do know is that every month,

54
00:01:33,993 --> 00:01:35,913
hundreds of thousands of American soldiers

55
00:01:35,913 --> 00:01:36,873
crossed the Atlantic

56
00:01:36,873 --> 00:01:38,976
And the virus went with them

57
00:01:39,126 --> 00:01:39,476
By May,

58
00:01:39,476 --> 00:01:41,076
it was in the trenches of France

59
00:01:41,076 --> 00:01:43,036
And it was about to get a completely wrong

60
00:01:43,036 --> 00:01:44,156
name

61
00:01:47,650 --> 00:01:48,800
In 1918,

62
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:50,200
most of Europe was at war,

63
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:52,121
and the press was censored

64
00:01:52,271 --> 00:01:54,541
No country at war wanted the enemy to know

65
00:01:54,541 --> 00:01:56,766
its soldiers were falling sick

66
00:01:56,916 --> 00:01:57,986
But Spain was neutral

67
00:01:57,986 --> 00:02:00,540
Its newspapers were free to report

68
00:02:00,690 --> 00:02:02,160
In late May 1918,

69
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:04,400
the papers in Madrid reported a strange

70
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:06,592
epidemic spreading through the city

71
00:02:06,742 --> 00:02:09,292
Then King Alfonso XIII himself fell ill

72
00:02:09,292 --> 00:02:11,577
The news went around the world

73
00:02:11,727 --> 00:02:13,997
Because the only reports came from Spain,

74
00:02:13,997 --> 00:02:15,737
many assumed it had started there

75
00:02:15,737 --> 00:02:18,234
The name Spanish flu was born

76
00:02:18,384 --> 00:02:18,854
The Spanish,

77
00:02:18,854 --> 00:02:19,494
for their part,

78
00:02:19,494 --> 00:02:20,814
called it the French flu,

79
00:02:20,814 --> 00:02:22,174
or the Naples Soldier,

80
00:02:22,174 --> 00:02:24,171
after a popular song

81
00:02:24,321 --> 00:02:24,911
By July,

82
00:02:24,911 --> 00:02:26,271
the first wave was fading

83
00:02:26,271 --> 00:02:28,169
Most of the sick recovered

84
00:02:28,319 --> 00:02:30,069
People breathed a sigh of relief,

85
00:02:30,069 --> 00:02:31,029
thinking it was over

86
00:02:31,029 --> 00:02:31,669
They were wrong

87
00:02:31,669 --> 00:02:33,749
The virus was changing

88
00:02:37,350 --> 00:02:39,300
In late August 1918,

89
00:02:39,300 --> 00:02:40,740
almost at the same moment,

90
00:02:40,740 --> 00:02:43,180
the disease exploded in three ports thousands

91
00:02:43,180 --> 00:02:44,940
of kilometres apart

92
00:02:45,245 --> 00:02:45,995
Brest in France,

93
00:02:45,995 --> 00:02:47,435
where American troops landed

94
00:02:47,435 --> 00:02:48,795
Freetown in Africa,

95
00:02:48,795 --> 00:02:50,475
a coaling station for warships

96
00:02:50,475 --> 00:02:51,195
And Boston,

97
00:02:51,195 --> 00:02:53,060
in the United States

98
00:02:53,210 --> 00:02:54,600
On August the twenty-seventh,

99
00:02:54,600 --> 00:02:55,720
1918,

100
00:02:55,720 --> 00:02:58,040
a few sailors in Boston reported sick

101
00:02:58,040 --> 00:02:59,040
Three days later,

102
00:02:59,040 --> 00:03:00,982
there were nearly sixty

103
00:03:01,132 --> 00:03:01,642
This time,

104
00:03:01,642 --> 00:03:03,835
it was no three-day fever

105
00:03:03,985 --> 00:03:05,694
Patients got worse frighteningly fast

106
00:03:05,694 --> 00:03:06,895
Many struggled to breathe,

107
00:03:06,895 --> 00:03:09,294
their skin turned bluish from lack of oxygen,

108
00:03:09,294 --> 00:03:11,694
and then came severe pneumonia

109
00:03:12,061 --> 00:03:13,731
Near Boston stood Camp Devens

110
00:03:13,731 --> 00:03:15,371
On September the twenty-second,

111
00:03:15,371 --> 00:03:16,531
1918,

112
00:03:16,531 --> 00:03:18,651
more than fifteen hundred soldiers reported

113
00:03:18,651 --> 00:03:20,541
sick in a single day

114
00:03:20,691 --> 00:03:21,841
By the end of September,

115
00:03:21,841 --> 00:03:24,281
the camp had more than fourteen thousand cases,

116
00:03:24,281 --> 00:03:26,881
and more than seven hundred men had died

117
00:03:27,254 --> 00:03:29,164
But what baffled the doctors most was not the

118
00:03:29,164 --> 00:03:29,524
speed

119
00:03:29,524 --> 00:03:31,924
It was who was dying

120
00:03:35,050 --> 00:03:35,380
Normally,

121
00:03:35,380 --> 00:03:37,520
flu is most dangerous for babies and the

122
00:03:37,520 --> 00:03:37,960
elderly

123
00:03:37,960 --> 00:03:39,000
Plot deaths by age,

124
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:40,822
and you get a U shape

125
00:03:40,972 --> 00:03:42,162
In 1918,

126
00:03:42,162 --> 00:03:44,442
that curve grew an extra peak in the middle

127
00:03:44,442 --> 00:03:46,398
It became a W

128
00:03:46,548 --> 00:03:48,578
About half of all the dead were aged twenty

129
00:03:48,578 --> 00:03:49,098
to forty,

130
00:03:49,098 --> 00:03:51,178
the very healthiest age

131
00:03:51,417 --> 00:03:51,887
Normally,

132
00:03:51,887 --> 00:03:54,140
fewer than one in every thousand people with

133
00:03:54,140 --> 00:03:54,727
flu dies

134
00:03:54,727 --> 00:03:55,967
In 1918,

135
00:03:55,967 --> 00:03:58,191
it was more than twenty-five

136
00:03:58,341 --> 00:03:58,341
Why?

137
00:03:58,341 --> 00:04:00,941
Scientists are still debating it today

138
00:04:01,186 --> 00:04:01,816
One theory:

139
00:04:01,816 --> 00:04:03,696
young immune systems overreacted,

140
00:04:03,696 --> 00:04:05,963
and damaged their own lungs

141
00:04:06,113 --> 00:04:06,503
Another:

142
00:04:06,503 --> 00:04:08,863
older people had met a similar flu many years

143
00:04:08,863 --> 00:04:09,183
before,

144
00:04:09,183 --> 00:04:11,226
and were partly protected

145
00:04:11,376 --> 00:04:13,726
But most deaths came from bacterial pneumonia,

146
00:04:13,726 --> 00:04:15,806
striking after the flu had weakened the lungs

147
00:04:15,806 --> 00:04:17,166
And in 1918,

148
00:04:17,166 --> 00:04:19,566
the world had no antibiotics

149
00:04:19,751 --> 00:04:20,221
No cure,

150
00:04:20,221 --> 00:04:21,101
no vaccine

151
00:04:21,101 --> 00:04:22,581
All people had were decisions

152
00:04:22,581 --> 00:04:25,181
And some decisions came at a terrible price

153
00:04:29,417 --> 00:04:30,167
Philadelphia,

154
00:04:30,167 --> 00:04:31,927
mid-September 1918

155
00:04:31,927 --> 00:04:34,527
The flu had reached the city's navy yard

156
00:04:34,828 --> 00:04:36,138
For September the twenty-eighth,

157
00:04:36,138 --> 00:04:37,738
a giant parade was planned,

158
00:04:37,738 --> 00:04:40,158
to get people to buy war bonds

159
00:04:40,308 --> 00:04:41,378
Doctors warned against it

160
00:04:41,378 --> 00:04:42,538
But Wilmer Krusen,

161
00:04:42,538 --> 00:04:44,418
the city's director of public health,

162
00:04:44,418 --> 00:04:46,350
let the parade go ahead

163
00:04:46,500 --> 00:04:48,450
Around two hundred thousand people packed the

164
00:04:48,450 --> 00:04:49,170
sidewalks,

165
00:04:49,170 --> 00:04:50,930
and the parade stretched for more than three

166
00:04:50,930 --> 00:04:52,050
kilometres

167
00:04:52,507 --> 00:04:53,857
Seventy-two hours later,

168
00:04:53,857 --> 00:04:56,297
every bed in all thirty-one of Philadelphia's

169
00:04:56,297 --> 00:04:58,003
hospitals was full

170
00:04:58,153 --> 00:04:59,743
In the first week of October alone,

171
00:04:59,743 --> 00:05:01,903
about two thousand six hundred people died

172
00:05:01,903 --> 00:05:02,703
The next week,

173
00:05:02,703 --> 00:05:05,259
more than four thousand five hundred

174
00:05:05,409 --> 00:05:06,239
Meanwhile in St

175
00:05:06,239 --> 00:05:06,599
Louis,

176
00:05:06,599 --> 00:05:08,719
health commissioner Max Starkloff did the

177
00:05:08,719 --> 00:05:09,359
opposite:

178
00:05:09,359 --> 00:05:10,959
he closed schools and theatres,

179
00:05:10,959 --> 00:05:12,339
and banned public gatherings,

180
00:05:12,339 --> 00:05:14,739
despite protests from business owners

181
00:05:15,247 --> 00:05:16,677
Researchers later worked it out:

182
00:05:16,677 --> 00:05:17,357
at the peak,

183
00:05:17,357 --> 00:05:19,557
Philadelphia's death rate was more than eight

184
00:05:19,557 --> 00:05:20,485
times that of St

185
00:05:20,485 --> 00:05:21,529
Louis

186
00:05:21,679 --> 00:05:23,589
Why was Philadelphia so reckless?

187
00:05:23,589 --> 00:05:25,909
Part of the answer lies in something even more

188
00:05:25,909 --> 00:05:27,229
dangerous than the virus:

189
00:05:27,229 --> 00:05:28,349
silence

190
00:05:32,117 --> 00:05:33,147
In 1918,

191
00:05:33,147 --> 00:05:35,033
the war came first

192
00:05:35,183 --> 00:05:35,833
In America,

193
00:05:35,833 --> 00:05:38,253
wartime laws could jail anyone accused of

194
00:05:38,253 --> 00:05:40,333
undermining the nation's morale

195
00:05:40,609 --> 00:05:42,559
Many officials insisted it was just ordinary

196
00:05:42,559 --> 00:05:42,919
flu

197
00:05:42,919 --> 00:05:44,559
One newspaper even advised:

198
00:05:44,559 --> 00:05:45,989
don't panic

199
00:05:46,139 --> 00:05:46,609
Meanwhile,

200
00:05:46,609 --> 00:05:48,409
troopships kept crossing the ocean,

201
00:05:48,409 --> 00:05:50,509
carrying the virus with them

202
00:05:50,659 --> 00:05:52,689
In the American army in Europe alone,

203
00:05:52,689 --> 00:05:54,849
more soldiers were hospitalised with flu than

204
00:05:54,849 --> 00:05:56,678
were wounded in battle

205
00:05:56,828 --> 00:05:58,058
On November the eleventh,

206
00:05:58,058 --> 00:05:59,098
1918,

207
00:05:59,098 --> 00:05:59,858
the war ended

208
00:05:59,858 --> 00:06:02,058
Millions poured into the streets to celebrate,

209
00:06:02,058 --> 00:06:03,818
hugging and dancing

210
00:06:03,996 --> 00:06:04,826
In many places,

211
00:06:04,826 --> 00:06:05,866
after those crowds,

212
00:06:05,866 --> 00:06:08,266
the epidemic flared up again

213
00:06:08,520 --> 00:06:09,350
With no medicine,

214
00:06:09,350 --> 00:06:11,550
city governments had just one weapon left:

215
00:06:11,550 --> 00:06:12,470
a piece of gauze

216
00:06:12,470 --> 00:06:15,070
And it was about to start another war

217
00:06:18,517 --> 00:06:19,467
San Francisco,

218
00:06:19,467 --> 00:06:21,187
late October 1918

219
00:06:21,187 --> 00:06:22,427
The city passed a law:

220
00:06:22,427 --> 00:06:25,027
anyone out in public must wear a mask

221
00:06:25,286 --> 00:06:27,196
Masks were made of four layers of gauze

222
00:06:27,196 --> 00:06:29,356
Anyone without one could be fined five dollars,

223
00:06:29,356 --> 00:06:31,932
or even spend a few days in jail

224
00:06:32,082 --> 00:06:33,872
Police arrested hundreds of people

225
00:06:33,872 --> 00:06:36,472
The papers called them mask slackers

226
00:06:36,893 --> 00:06:38,203
On November the twenty-first,

227
00:06:38,203 --> 00:06:39,243
1918,

228
00:06:39,243 --> 00:06:40,323
with cases falling,

229
00:06:40,323 --> 00:06:41,403
the order was lifted

230
00:06:41,403 --> 00:06:44,003
The whole city took off its masks to celebrate

231
00:06:44,629 --> 00:06:45,859
Less than two months later,

232
00:06:45,859 --> 00:06:47,179
the epidemic came back

233
00:06:47,179 --> 00:06:48,819
In January 1919,

234
00:06:48,819 --> 00:06:51,147
the city ordered masks again

235
00:06:51,297 --> 00:06:51,767
This time,

236
00:06:51,767 --> 00:06:52,607
many refused

237
00:06:52,607 --> 00:06:55,006
They founded the Anti-Mask League

238
00:06:55,156 --> 00:06:56,666
On January the twenty-fifth,

239
00:06:56,666 --> 00:06:57,826
1919,

240
00:06:57,826 --> 00:07:00,106
thousands packed into a roller-skating rink to

241
00:07:00,106 --> 00:07:01,226
protest

242
00:07:01,453 --> 00:07:02,443
In early February,

243
00:07:02,443 --> 00:07:04,952
the second mask order was repealed

244
00:07:05,102 --> 00:07:06,972
While big cities argued about masks,

245
00:07:06,972 --> 00:07:08,332
in the middle of the Pacific,

246
00:07:08,332 --> 00:07:10,332
two neighbouring islands were about to choose

247
00:07:10,332 --> 00:07:11,772
opposite paths

248
00:07:15,750 --> 00:07:16,260
Samoa,

249
00:07:16,260 --> 00:07:17,540
in the middle of the Pacific

250
00:07:17,540 --> 00:07:19,460
Two island groups side by side,

251
00:07:19,460 --> 00:07:20,180
one people,

252
00:07:20,180 --> 00:07:21,620
one language

253
00:07:21,823 --> 00:07:23,333
The west was run by New Zealand

254
00:07:23,333 --> 00:07:25,516
The east was American Samoa

255
00:07:25,666 --> 00:07:26,776
On November the seventh,

256
00:07:26,776 --> 00:07:28,016
1918,

257
00:07:28,016 --> 00:07:30,976
the ship Talune from Auckland docked at Apia,

258
00:07:30,976 --> 00:07:32,056
in Western Samoa

259
00:07:32,056 --> 00:07:34,332
Some of its passengers were sick

260
00:07:34,482 --> 00:07:35,952
The ship was not quarantined

261
00:07:35,952 --> 00:07:38,552
The passengers went ashore as usual

262
00:07:39,037 --> 00:07:39,627
Within weeks,

263
00:07:39,627 --> 00:07:41,507
the disease swept through every village

264
00:07:41,507 --> 00:07:43,747
About eight thousand five hundred people died,

265
00:07:43,747 --> 00:07:45,667
nearly a fifth of the population,

266
00:07:45,667 --> 00:07:48,267
the highest rate in the world in the pandemic

267
00:07:48,982 --> 00:07:50,492
Less than a hundred kilometres away,

268
00:07:50,492 --> 00:07:52,292
the governor of American Samoa,

269
00:07:52,292 --> 00:07:53,292
John Poyer,

270
00:07:53,292 --> 00:07:55,605
heard the news on the radio

271
00:07:55,755 --> 00:07:57,145
He sealed the harbour at once

272
00:07:57,145 --> 00:07:58,265
No ship could land

273
00:07:58,265 --> 00:08:00,845
Even the mail was collected out at sea

274
00:08:00,995 --> 00:08:02,385
The people and their chiefs backed the

275
00:08:02,385 --> 00:08:02,945
quarantine

276
00:08:02,945 --> 00:08:03,625
The result:

277
00:08:03,625 --> 00:08:06,193
American Samoa had not a single death from the

278
00:08:06,193 --> 00:08:07,313
flu

279
00:08:07,492 --> 00:08:09,562
Poyer even offered to help Western Samoa

280
00:08:09,562 --> 00:08:10,682
According to the records,

281
00:08:10,682 --> 00:08:12,756
the offer was refused

282
00:08:12,906 --> 00:08:14,616
But not every place had a harbour it could

283
00:08:14,616 --> 00:08:15,736
close

284
00:08:19,283 --> 00:08:20,593
In June 1918,

285
00:08:20,593 --> 00:08:22,873
the virus arrived by ship in Bombay,

286
00:08:22,873 --> 00:08:24,242
in India

287
00:08:24,392 --> 00:08:26,022
The second wave struck in the autumn,

288
00:08:26,022 --> 00:08:28,622
just as drought and crop failure hit

289
00:08:28,781 --> 00:08:31,131
India lost at least twelve million people,

290
00:08:31,131 --> 00:08:32,371
more than any other country

291
00:08:32,371 --> 00:08:34,211
It was the only decade in which India's

292
00:08:34,211 --> 00:08:35,651
population fell

293
00:08:36,118 --> 00:08:36,788
In Alaska,

294
00:08:36,788 --> 00:08:39,188
some villages lost almost all of their adults

295
00:08:39,188 --> 00:08:41,134
in just a few days

296
00:08:41,284 --> 00:08:41,874
Schools,

297
00:08:41,874 --> 00:08:43,394
theatres and churches closed

298
00:08:43,394 --> 00:08:45,914
Telephone exchanges ran short of operators,

299
00:08:45,914 --> 00:08:47,918
because the staff were sick

300
00:08:48,068 --> 00:08:48,818
No one was spared

301
00:08:48,818 --> 00:08:51,258
The painter Egon Schiele died at twenty-eight,

302
00:08:51,258 --> 00:08:53,858
just three days after his pregnant wife

303
00:08:54,279 --> 00:08:55,789
The painter Edvard Munch caught it,

304
00:08:55,789 --> 00:08:57,949
and painted a portrait of himself in the grip

305
00:08:57,949 --> 00:08:59,219
of the flu

306
00:08:59,369 --> 00:09:01,719
A teenager named Walt Disney caught it too,

307
00:09:01,719 --> 00:09:02,879
and luckily recovered

308
00:09:02,879 --> 00:09:05,079
A young politician named Franklin Roosevelt

309
00:09:05,079 --> 00:09:07,679
fell seriously ill on a ship home from Europe

310
00:09:08,196 --> 00:09:09,666
By the end of 1918,

311
00:09:09,666 --> 00:09:10,986
the second wave was easing

312
00:09:10,986 --> 00:09:13,586
But the virus had not had its last word

313
00:09:17,583 --> 00:09:18,933
In early 1919,

314
00:09:18,933 --> 00:09:21,093
a third wave swept through many places

315
00:09:21,093 --> 00:09:22,333
Milder than the autumn,

316
00:09:22,333 --> 00:09:24,028
but still deadly

317
00:09:24,178 --> 00:09:26,768
Australia had quarantined ships since October

318
00:09:26,768 --> 00:09:27,648
1918,

319
00:09:27,648 --> 00:09:28,848
and escaped the worst wave

320
00:09:28,848 --> 00:09:30,488
But in early 1919,

321
00:09:30,488 --> 00:09:32,682
the virus still got in

322
00:09:32,832 --> 00:09:34,382
In April 1919,

323
00:09:34,382 --> 00:09:36,462
in the middle of the Paris peace conference,

324
00:09:36,462 --> 00:09:38,902
American President Woodrow Wilson suddenly fell

325
00:09:38,902 --> 00:09:39,182
ill

326
00:09:39,182 --> 00:09:41,782
Many historians believe it was the flu

327
00:09:41,961 --> 00:09:43,791
Some argue the illness exhausted him,

328
00:09:43,791 --> 00:09:45,191
and made him give more ground at the

329
00:09:45,191 --> 00:09:46,151
negotiating table

330
00:09:46,151 --> 00:09:48,166
That is still debated today

331
00:09:48,316 --> 00:09:49,146
In some places,

332
00:09:49,146 --> 00:09:50,546
there was even a fourth wave,

333
00:09:50,546 --> 00:09:51,986
in 1920

334
00:09:52,488 --> 00:09:54,318
Then the virus slowly grew milder

335
00:09:54,318 --> 00:09:56,398
Most of humanity had been infected and had

336
00:09:56,398 --> 00:09:56,918
immunity

337
00:09:56,918 --> 00:09:59,076
It became a seasonal flu

338
00:09:59,226 --> 00:09:59,736
In total,

339
00:09:59,736 --> 00:10:02,056
around five hundred million people fell ill

340
00:10:02,056 --> 00:10:04,336
Estimates of the dead range from seventeen to a

341
00:10:04,336 --> 00:10:04,976
hundred million

342
00:10:04,976 --> 00:10:07,256
The figure most often quoted is about fifty

343
00:10:07,256 --> 00:10:08,376
million

344
00:10:08,653 --> 00:10:10,003
In the United States alone,

345
00:10:10,003 --> 00:10:12,123
about six hundred and seventy-five thousand

346
00:10:12,123 --> 00:10:12,643
people died

347
00:10:12,643 --> 00:10:16,043
Average life expectancy in 1918 fell by about

348
00:10:16,043 --> 00:10:17,483
twelve years

349
00:10:17,828 --> 00:10:19,778
But one question was still unanswered:

350
00:10:19,778 --> 00:10:20,098
who,

351
00:10:20,098 --> 00:10:20,818
in the end,

352
00:10:20,818 --> 00:10:22,578
was the killer?

353
00:10:25,950 --> 00:10:26,980
In 1918,

354
00:10:26,980 --> 00:10:29,620
doctors had no idea that flu viruses even

355
00:10:29,620 --> 00:10:30,740
existed

356
00:10:30,920 --> 00:10:32,883
They blamed a bacterium known as Pfeiffer's

357
00:10:32,883 --> 00:10:33,230
bacillus

358
00:10:33,230 --> 00:10:35,470
Many anti-bacterial vaccines were given,

359
00:10:35,470 --> 00:10:37,879
but they did not stop the flu

360
00:10:38,029 --> 00:10:41,299
Only in 1933 did scientists in London find the

361
00:10:41,299 --> 00:10:41,939
real culprit:

362
00:10:41,939 --> 00:10:42,659
a virus

363
00:10:42,659 --> 00:10:45,221
They ran their experiments on ferrets

364
00:10:45,371 --> 00:10:47,161
Legend has it that a sick ferret sneezed in

365
00:10:47,161 --> 00:10:48,121
a scientist's face

366
00:10:48,121 --> 00:10:49,321
A few days later,

367
00:10:49,321 --> 00:10:51,227
he had the flu too

368
00:10:51,377 --> 00:10:53,927
But the 1918 virus had vanished long ago

369
00:10:53,927 --> 00:10:56,165
How could anyone find it again?

370
00:10:56,315 --> 00:10:57,505
In 1951,

371
00:10:57,505 --> 00:11:00,265
a young Swedish student named Johan Hultin

372
00:11:00,265 --> 00:11:02,145
travelled to the village of Brevig Mission in

373
00:11:02,145 --> 00:11:02,825
Alaska,

374
00:11:02,825 --> 00:11:04,825
where the flu had killed seventy-two of its

375
00:11:04,825 --> 00:11:06,265
eighty villagers

376
00:11:06,629 --> 00:11:08,219
With the permission of the village elders,

377
00:11:08,219 --> 00:11:10,259
he searched for tissue preserved by the

378
00:11:10,259 --> 00:11:10,659
permafrost

379
00:11:10,659 --> 00:11:13,259
But he could not grow any live virus

380
00:11:13,483 --> 00:11:14,766
Forty-six years later,

381
00:11:14,766 --> 00:11:16,273
Hultin was seventy-two

382
00:11:16,273 --> 00:11:18,180
He read a paper by the scientist Jeffery

383
00:11:18,180 --> 00:11:19,433
Taubenberger,

384
00:11:19,433 --> 00:11:22,073
who was decoding the 1918 virus from army

385
00:11:22,073 --> 00:11:23,513
tissue archives

386
00:11:24,064 --> 00:11:24,934
Paying his own way,

387
00:11:24,934 --> 00:11:26,494
he went back to Brevig Mission,

388
00:11:26,494 --> 00:11:28,334
and this time he brought back a precious

389
00:11:28,334 --> 00:11:29,410
sample

390
00:11:29,560 --> 00:11:31,030
In 2005,

391
00:11:31,030 --> 00:11:33,270
scientists published the complete genome of the

392
00:11:33,270 --> 00:11:34,630
1918 virus,

393
00:11:34,630 --> 00:11:36,950
and rebuilt it in a high-security laboratory

394
00:11:36,950 --> 00:11:39,550
It had come from a bird flu virus

395
00:11:39,850 --> 00:11:42,040
And they found something even more astonishing:

396
00:11:42,040 --> 00:11:44,640
its descendants are still all around us

397
00:11:48,717 --> 00:11:51,347
The 1918 virus has been called the mother of

398
00:11:51,347 --> 00:11:52,787
all pandemics

399
00:11:53,164 --> 00:11:54,954
Its descendants still circulate today,

400
00:11:54,954 --> 00:11:57,234
and passed genes on to the flu strains behind

401
00:11:57,234 --> 00:11:59,314
the pandemics of 1957,

402
00:11:59,314 --> 00:12:01,074
1968 and 2009

403
00:12:02,723 --> 00:12:03,313
Every year,

404
00:12:03,313 --> 00:12:05,633
labs around the world track flu viruses to

405
00:12:05,633 --> 00:12:08,233
choose what goes into next season's vaccine

406
00:12:08,892 --> 00:12:10,722
The numbers from Philadelphia and St

407
00:12:10,722 --> 00:12:12,322
Louis became a classic lesson:

408
00:12:12,322 --> 00:12:14,402
acting early saves lives

409
00:12:14,736 --> 00:12:16,126
In 2007,

410
00:12:16,126 --> 00:12:19,046
researchers used that very 1918 data to build

411
00:12:19,046 --> 00:12:20,486
pandemic plans

412
00:12:20,940 --> 00:12:21,730
In 2020,

413
00:12:21,730 --> 00:12:23,210
when COVID-19 appeared,

414
00:12:23,210 --> 00:12:25,450
the phrase flatten the curve echoed around the

415
00:12:25,450 --> 00:12:25,690
world,

416
00:12:25,690 --> 00:12:28,290
and the charts from 1918 came out once again

417
00:12:29,431 --> 00:12:30,341
A century on,

418
00:12:30,341 --> 00:12:31,821
the lesson is the same:

419
00:12:31,821 --> 00:12:33,021
honest information,

420
00:12:33,021 --> 00:12:33,861
early action,

421
00:12:33,861 --> 00:12:36,007
and caring for one another

422
00:12:36,157 --> 00:12:37,147
Viruses can change

423
00:12:37,147 --> 00:12:39,747
But how we face them is up to us

424
00:12:40,683 --> 00:12:42,993
The Spanish flu is the story of an invisible

425
00:12:42,993 --> 00:12:43,473
virus,

426
00:12:43,473 --> 00:12:45,565
and of very human decisions

427
00:12:45,715 --> 00:12:47,465
If you had been Philadelphia's health director

428
00:12:47,465 --> 00:12:48,705
in 1918,

429
00:12:48,705 --> 00:12:50,145
would you have cancelled the parade?

430
00:12:50,145 --> 00:12:51,904
Tell me in the comments

